  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The aurora painted Xouhoulong's plaza in bands of violet and ice-blue, each crystal spire catching the light at precisely the angle he'd calculated, funneling it down through the concentric trade-floors where three hundred penguins stood in the glow and pretended they didn't hate each other.
    
    Lianoshing mingi'Ward watched from the eastern archway. She'd carried his schematics for two years, fetched his aurora-glass, recalibrated the refraction angles when the crystal-cutters botched them. She knew the plaza's geometry better than anyone alive except its maker—knew, for instance, that the central dais sat six degrees off true north because Xouhoulong had tilted it toward the Nightshelf tunnels. A courtesy. An invitation. A lie dressed as architecture.
    
    He stood on that dais now, circlet blazing, white robes catching the aurora-light so he seemed to radiate it himself. Still luminous. The scars on his flippers were hidden beneath formal silk, those fine white lines across the webbing where crystal-conduction had burned too hot before he'd learned restraint. He'd mastered everything since. That was the trouble.
    
    "The Accord holds," he told the assembly. His voice carried the way the plaza was designed to carry it—effortlessly, to every tier. "Seven renegotiations. Seven times we chose survival over pride. The fire-cores flow. The aurora-crystals descend. What the surface grows, the deep sustains."
    
    Applause from the Aurora Stack delegates. Silence from the Nightshelf contingent—but the right kind of silence, the kind that meant consent without the indignity of enthusiasm. In the third-tier gallery, a clutch of ☽'ofund' Guild merchants watched with the particular stillness of people counting margins. Their amulets of fire-core authentication glinted dully. One of them—a scarred veteran named Niaecum~—leaned to his companion and said something that made her cover her beak.
    
    Lianoshing caught the flash of Niaecum~'s teeth. She'd seen that expression before: the look of someone profiting from the gap between what was said and what was done.
    
    Mandatum∴absconditu. The doctrine nobody named in public, the permission slip that made the whole machine function. Two economies, two governments, two truths. The Accord governed the visible trade; beneath it, ☽'ofund' moved what actually mattered through tunnels so old the ice remembered their footsteps. Xouhoulong knew. Of course he knew. He'd built the antechamber behind the dais specifically for the conversations that couldn't happen on the floor—small enough that two penguins couldn't avoid each other, large enough that a third could press their beak to the wall and hear everything.
    
    He'd designed the architecture of deception and called it diplomacy.
    
    The aurora shifted to green—that particular green, the shade the elders flinched from—and for an instant Lianoshing saw something wrong in the way the light hit the lowest galleries. A stutter. A refusal. As if the ice itself had blinked.
    
    Xouhoulong didn't notice. He was already turning to greet the ☽'ofund' delegation, his movements precise, measured, the joints stiff with cold that shouldn't have touched them. Still luminous, but fractured where it mattered.
    
    ---
    
    The fire-core shortage announced itself not with alarms but with arithmetic.
    
    Lianoshing laid the tallies on his desk in the antechamber—cramped, cold, the walls sweating condensation that froze into patterns she tried not to read. Xouhoulong studied the numbers the way he studied everything: thoroughly, silently, with the particular focus of someone who had already calculated the answer and was checking the universe's work.
    
    "Three weeks," he said.
    
    "At current distribution. Less if the deep veins collapse again."
    
    "They'll collapse." Not a guess. The Nightshelf miners were pulling cores from depths that groaned when you listened too long, the kind of depths where the ice remembered pressure and resented company. Niaecum~ had told her this in the third-tier gallery, laughing, because ☽'ofund' traders laughed at everything—*Only Losses Found*, they called themselves, and meant it.
    
    Xouhoulong touched the Directrix Mirror. It sat on his desk like a paperweight from a better era—palm-sized, aurora-glass face showing the two colonies in profile, Aurora Stack's spires refracting light into the Nightshelf's vents below. Beautiful. Deliberately beautiful. The kind of object designed to make what it authenticated feel inevitable. Beside it sat the orca carving—smaller than his flipper, rendered in bone by hands that had no fingers. He'd taken it from a Wake-Singer corpse during the Vum∴tenebra. Kept it because it reminded him what waited in the deep. Kept it because some nights, in the hour before dawn when the colony slept and the aurora dimmed to whispers, Lianoshing had seen him staring at it as though it were staring back.
    
    "The orca clans have made an offer," he said.
    
    Lianoshing's flippers went cold. Not the clean bite of surface cold—pressure-cold, the kind that told you something was wrong before you understood what.
    
    "The Midnight Claws. They'll protect the harvesting fleets in exchange for access to the deep tunnels." He turned the Mirror in his flippers. Light caught the aurora-glass; the two colonies seemed to shift, to lean toward each other. "Vel'keth brought the terms himself. Swimming right up to the shore-wall, bold as hunger."
    
    "Vel'keth is Wake-Singer."
    
    "Vel'keth is a pragmatist who wears a bone headdress carved from his grandmother while the bone was still warm. And he commands forty raiders who can keep our fleets alive through the season." Xouhoulong set the Mirror down. "The Accord is dying, Lianoshing. Not from any single wound—from seven. Seven renegotiations. Seven times I cut away what was necessary to preserve what was essential, and now the essential is bleeding out on the trade-floor and nobody will say it because Mandatum∴absconditu has made silence sacred."
    
    She heard it then—the crack in his voice that no architecture could hide. He wasn't justifying. He was confessing. And confessing, in his world, was the same as deciding.
    
    "Selka would counsel against it," Lianoshing said.
    
    His flippers stopped moving. High-Beak Auditor Selka, his predecessor, his mentor, the penguin who'd made him promise transparency the day she'd handed him the circlet. Selka, who worked in the eastern galleries every morning because she believed a leader should see the sun before making decisions that affected those who couldn't.
    
    "Selka would counsel patience," Xouhoulong said. "And three weeks from now, when the last fire-core gutters out and the heating braziers go dark, patience would hold a memorial vigil for the frozen dead and call it wisdom." He picked up the Mirror again. "I am going to press this to the Foedus∴vincu. The orca terms. The pact."
    
    "And the ☽'ofund' Guild?"
    
    "Will pretend they don't know, as they pretend about everything. Niaecum~ has already arranged the contraband routes the Claws will need. The doctrine permits it. The doctrine demands it."
    
    He pressed the Directrix Mirror to the document.
    
    The aurora-glass sang once—a single note, clear and high—and it echoed longer than physics allowed, ringing off the antechamber walls, vibrating in Lianoshing's chest. She felt the ice shift beneath her feet. Not much. A settling. The kind of sound a structure makes when a load-bearing wall accepts weight it was never designed to carry.
    
    Xouhoulong set the Mirror beside the Foedus∴vincu. His flippers left condensation on the glass. The two colonies in profile stared at each other across a surface that was, for now, unbroken.
    
    "There," he said. "It's done."
    
    He didn't look at her when he said it. He was staring at the wall—at the space where Selka's commendation plaque had hung before he'd moved it to the vault because he couldn't work beneath her gaze.
    
    ---
    
    The research chambers occupied the lowest level of the Hearth Plaza, in galleries Xouhoulong had sealed before the first merchant ever crossed the threshold.
    
    Lianoshing shouldn't have been there. She'd followed instructions carved into ice near the antechamber—Xouhoulong's hand, unmistakable, telling her to bring calibration instruments to a sublevel that appeared on no official schematic. Spiral channels cut into ancient ice. Fire-core braziers positioned to maintain precise temperatures. And somewhere in the deep, the distant pressure-song of something that had been sleeping since before penguins walked the Berg.
    
    Lovumruius~ Domina was already inside, her guild-marks catching brazier-light—three fire-cores fused into her chest-feathers, the old way, before they'd learned that hurt. Six ☽'ofund' researchers and two of Xouhoulong's own aurora-readers stood at the carved spirals, collaborating in a space that existed in no official record. The Nightshelf doctrine made it simple: two economies, two governments, two truths. What happened below the trade-floors served the colony's survival. That was enough.
    
    It wasn't enough.
    
    Kesho (twice-blessed) qi'Vault was the first to bleed.
    
    Lianoshing watched from the observation gallery as Kesho pressed her flipper to the carved spiral and sang the preliminary invocation. The ice groaned. Frost bloomed across surfaces that had been carefully heated to prevent adhesion. And something in the deep answered—not words, not sound, but pressure, the weight of centuries compressing into a single moment of *attention*.
    
    "It's listening," Kesho breathed. "The ice—it's actually—"
    
    The aurora-light in the chamber stuttered. Once; twice; then a rhythm that matched no mathematical sequence Xouhoulong had ever designed. Lianoshing felt the pressure-cold—the kind that preceded knowing—settle into her bones. The eastern spire above them dimmed, and its glow never quite corrected afterward.
    
    Xouhoulong, standing rigid at the observation rail, told himself it was interference. Residual harmonics. Something that could be calibrated.
    
    He didn't ask what the ice was listening to.
    
    The second researcher, Morrund (ash-born) teir'Cinder, bled into the channels three days later. The spirals were deeper now—the ice remembered the pressure-song and had begun to carve itself, extending in directions the researchers hadn't planned. Morrund's blood ran darker than it should have. His eyes went distant for a long moment before he came back shaking, his flippers trembling against ice that felt warmer than the fire-cores could account for.
    
    "I saw something," he said. "In the pressure. A shape. Larger than—"
    
    "Focus on the data," Xouhoulong said. "Measurements first. Interpretations later."
    
    But Morrund couldn't focus. None of them could. The ice was remembering too well, too fast, calling up things that shouldn't have left impressions—the Vum∴tenebra's mass drownings, the moment when a mystic first touched Thanksgiving ice and felt every starvation-death at once. The spirals reached through the ice like veins seeking a heart.
    
    Lovumruius~ came to the observation gallery on the nineteenth day. Her expression was the careful nothing of a merchant who'd already calculated losses.
    
    "They know," she said. "The Wake-Singers. Someone told them, or the ice told them, or—" She cut herself off. "They're coming through tunnels we didn't know existed. Old passages. Before the Thaw."
    
    "Withdraw the researchers. Seal the chambers."
    
    "Too late."
    
    The chamber below went silent. Not quiet—*silent*, the absolute absence of sound that only happened when the ice itself stopped vibrating. Lianoshing looked down through the observation crystal and saw Kesho and Morrund standing perfectly still, their eyes fixed on a section of wall that had cracked open without any carving to guide it.
    
    Something moved in the darkness beyond.
    
    The aurora-light failed. There were sounds—not screams, not words, frequencies that made Lianoshing's heart skip and the observation crystal groan. When the light returned six hours later, Kesho and Morrund were gone. The spiral channels had extended through the breach, reaching toward the deep tunnels like veins seeking a heart.
    
    Lovumruius~ left that night. The guild withdrew with the efficiency of merchants cutting losses, severing every connection before the scandal could spread. Her parting message was carved into the ice near the sealed gallery: *Only Losses Found.*
    
    Niaecum~ stayed. Niaecum~ laughed.
    
    ---
    
    Three weeks after the pact, the Calving tore the Berg's eastern face open and High-Beak Auditor Selka vanished with it.
    
    Not vanished. Died. Crushed under a hundred tons of fracturing ice while cataloguing trade-receipts in the galleries where she went every morning to see the sun. They found her ledger three days afterward, waterlogged, the last entry a note about fire-core discrepancies she'd been meaning to raise with Xouhoulong. She'd noticed. Of course she'd noticed.
    
    Lianoshing carried the news to him. He received it standing, perfectly still, the circlet dimming as the aurora guttered above a Berg that was suddenly, violently smaller. He said: "Casualties?" She gave him numbers. He said: "The plaza?" She told him: damaged, the lower galleries flooded, corruption seeping through the breach-points in ice already stressed by the weight of the research he'd hidden in its foundations. He said: "The Accord?" She told him: holding. Barely. The ☽'ofund' Guild was demanding renegotiation. Eighth time. The Nightshelf miners had sealed three tunnels the Midnight Claws had used; the orcas were refusing to vacate the others.
    
    What she didn't tell him—what she couldn't, because the words felt like the pressure-cold that preceded a cascade—was that the Wake-Singers had found the Fissured Smelt. That the tunnels he'd opened for the Midnight Claws connected, through routes no cartographer had mapped, to the deep passages where the corruption was thickest. That Vel'keth had shed the pretense of pragmatism and descended through the corruption-scar with forty chanters who sang in frequencies that made penguins' hearts skip and ice-sheets groan.
    
    That came later. Niaecum~ brought it, laughing—because ☽'ofund' traders laughed at everything—standing in the third-tier gallery of a plaza that now bore char-marks where they'd tried to burn the corruption out. The Wake-Singers had taken sections of the Hearth Plaza itself. Xouhoulong's monument. His mathematical precision turned into an incursion corridor because the geometry that funneled light also funneled sound, and the Wake-Singers' hymns propagated through his crystal spires with devastating efficiency.
    
    He sealed the southern gallery personally. Lianoshing watched him work—aurora-glass and ice-weld, his flippers bleeding from cuts he didn't acknowledge, the scars of old burns splitting open under new pressure. When he finished, the aurora-light wouldn't enter that section. It refused. As if the ice remembered what had been authenticated there and wanted no part of it.
    
    "Niaecum~ says the guild charter is under review," Lianoshing told him afterward, in the antechamber that now smelled of char and grief. "The Shadow Parliament won't acknowledge the Foedus∴vincu. They're calling the pact unauthorized."
    
    "The Directrix Mirror authorized it."
    
    "The Mirror is cracked, sir."
    
    She'd seen it herself. When they'd retrieved the seal from the flooded archive, a jagged fissure split its aurora-glass face in two. The crack cut directly through the image of the two colonies. Separating them. Assessor Zheishongfeihua had examined it once and wouldn't speak for a month.
    
    Xouhoulong looked at his flippers. Blood on silk. Old burns opened to new air. On the desk, the orca carving sat where it always sat. It seemed heavier now, its bone surface catching the brazier-light in ways that made the carved shape beneath appear to move. Vast. Patient. Watching with the particular attention of something that had already won.
    
    "Then we authenticate with what we have," he said. "Cracked across its face or not."
    
    ---
    
    Grand Auditor Qeihong arrived with the efficiency of authority that has finally noticed what it should have prevented.
    
    The investigation lasted eight days. Qeihong catalogued every deviation: sealed galleries that violated charter terms, symbols carved into ice that weren't Aurora Stack sacred geometry, accommodations with Nightshelf doctrines the Accord didn't recognize. He interviewed survivors who wouldn't meet his eyes. He examined the eastern spire's foundation, where defensive imprints had calcified into something that pulsed with a rhythm like a damaged heartbeat. He examined the research chambers—the spirals still extending, still reaching, though no one fed them.
    
    Xouhoulong cooperated. There was nothing else to do.
    
    "The Directrix Mirror authenticated these chambers," Qeihong said, holding the seal that had blessed seven cycles of secret work. "Under what authority did you commission research into Wake-Singer pressure-magic?"
    
    "Mandatum∴absconditu."
    
    "That doctrine governs Nightshelf internal affairs. You are Light-Regent of Aurora Stack."
    
    "I was protecting the colony."
    
    "You were protecting your vision of the colony." Qeihong set the Mirror on the table between them. Its aurora-glass face caught the light from the single brazier—fire-core warmth borrowed from the Nightshelf, because neither colony could survive without the other's resource. "There's a difference, Light-Regent. The ice knows which one matters."
    
    Lianoshing was not permitted in the room for the displacement. She heard the seal pressed to the document transferring Light-Regency to Qeihong. She did not hear the Mirror crack further, but when the witnesses retrieved it from the table, the fissure had deepened—a second line branching from the first, bisecting what remained.
    
    ---
    
    Thanksgiving came with the aurora's peak, and the dead outnumbered the living who gathered.
    
    Lianoshing stood in the upper gallery—one of the surviving ones, the crystal spires still catching light the way he'd designed them, painting the delegates in shifting bands of blue and violet. Beautiful. Functional. Wrong. The rhythm stuttered where the sealed sections interrupted the geometry. Below, traders argued in three dialects on the scarred trade-floors. Fire-cores from the Nightshelf glowed in every flipper; the Accord demanded this, and neither side admitted it was the only warmth that mattered.
    
    Xouhoulong stood on the central dais. He held no Mirror—it sat in the Wane☽ Oncea vault, cracked, functional, untouched. He held no document. He held a single fire-core that someone had left at his memorial plaque; it had been there so long it had begun to fuse into the ice, and he'd broken it free that morning with his bare flippers. His circlet caught the aurora, but the light moved through him now instead of from him. Still luminous. Still commanding. Still believing—
    
    No. Lianoshing saw the moment it left him: belief. Not dramatically, not the way the stories would tell it later. A settling. The sound a structure makes when it stops carrying weight.
    
    He looked at where the southern gallery had been—sealed, dark, the aurora refusing to touch it—and his flippers turned the fire-core over and over. Somewhere in those sealed corridors, the ice still replayed what it had witnessed: Kesho and Morrund standing in spirals that reached for something vast in the deep. Their eyes finding no one. Their beaks moving in frequencies no one could hear. Memory-impressions that the ice would carry long after the living forgot why they mattered.
    
    "The Accord holds," he said to the assembly. His voice still carried. The plaza was still designed for it.
    
    But his tone had changed. The seven renegotiations. The seven cuts. The fire-core scarcity that had pushed him to the pact, the pact that had opened the tunnels, the tunnels that had brought the Wake-Singers, the Wake-Singers who had scarred the plaza he'd built to hold everything together. Each step reasonable. Each step inevitable. Each step the expression of a penguin who believed that secrecy and control could substitute for trust, that managing the gap between what was said and what was done was the same as closing it.
    
    Mandatum∴absconditu. The doctrine that made hiding from truth a virtue. He'd breathed it like cold air since the day he'd taken the circlet from Selka's flippers, and it had kept him alive, kept the colony alive, kept the Accord stumbling forward through seven bleedings—until the truth came anyway, on frequencies that made bones ache, through tunnels he'd signed open with a seal that sang once and then broke.
    
    "I built this plaza," he said. Not to the assembly. To the ice. "I angled every spire. I calculated every refraction. I designed the antechamber where the real decisions happened, and I called it statecraft, and it was—" He stopped. Turned the fire-core in his flippers.
    
    The ice wept in specific places. Literal water that tasted like salt and old grief. The younger penguins reached for it, and the elders pulled them back, and Xouhoulong watched his legacy dissolve into tears the colony couldn't drink.
    
    He set the fire-core on the dais. It flickered—months of warmth still in it, stubborn, refusing to die the way fire-cores refused to die until they didn't.
    
    "Grand Auditor Qeihong will speak to the distribution schedule," he said. His voice carried. It always would. The plaza was built for it.
    
    He stepped down from the dais with the measured grace of someone who'd already calculated the cost, and he walked toward the sealed southern gallery where the aurora would not go, and he stood before the darkness his architecture had made, and he did not move for a very long time.
    
    In the third-tier gallery, Niaecum~ laughed at something. The sound echoed through crystal spires that were still, after everything, mathematically perfect. *Only Losses Found.* The guild's motto carried further than it should have, refracted through geometry designed to make every voice matter, and it landed in the silence around Xouhoulong like a verdict.
    
    Lianoshing stayed at the eastern archway. The aurora shifted. That particular shade of green. She did not flinch, because she was young enough not to remember the night they decided to eat the memorial tokens, but old enough now to understand why the elders did.
    
    She would remember this. All of it. The way the light moved through him at the end. The fire-core fusing into the ice of the dais. The cracked seal in its vault, authenticating nothing. The orca carving on his desk that watched with the patience of deep water. The researchers who would never return from the spirals. The plaza that worked—after a fashion—because survival doesn't care about meaning.
    
    What died with Xouhoulong wasn't just a leader but the possibility that the colonies could have unified through wisdom rather than catastrophe, that the ice could have been taught to remember hope instead of pain. The crack across the Mirror's face. The cathedral still standing, corrupted at its foundations. The hymns still propagating through crystal that couldn't stop being beautiful.
    
    Someone had scratched initials into his memorial plaque. She never found out whose.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771578180031_0", anchor: "Seven renegotiations. Seven times we chose survival over pride", text: "Seven. I found three secret drafts in administrative ice predating the public Accord, and those are only the ones that survived freezing. The actual count depends on whether you classify Mandatum∴absconditu amendments as renegotiations or quiet capitulations. I stopped counting at eleven. But Xouhoulong was a better archivist than most politicians, and seven is the kind of number a leader uses when he wants the audience to feel the weight without doing the arithmetic. I have chosen my own convenient numbers. I recognize the craft.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771578180031_1", anchor: "sat six degrees off true north because Xouhoulong had tilted it toward the Nightshelf tunnels", text: "I annotated this tilt in the Hearth Plaza entry and called it an intelligence post. Lianoshing names the exact angle and calls it a lie. We were both right, and neither of us was generous.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578180031_2", anchor: "Swimming right up to the shore-wall, bold as hunger", text: "Vel'keth commands forty raiders drilled in standard orca assault doctrine — shore-breakers to split the ice, herders to funnel targets into kill-zones, finishers waiting below escape routes, hunt-songs coordinating at frequencies that crack bone. He could have sent wave-breakers through that shore-wall. Instead he swam up alone and talked. The chronicler reads boldness. I read a military mind that understood a signed pact achieves what a raid cannot: permanent tunnel access without seasonal casualties. Orca are faster, stronger, patient enough to wait for mistakes — and, evidently, intelligent enough to recognize when a politician will make them for you.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771578180031_3", anchor: "the kind of depths where the ice remembered pressure and resented company", text: "'Resented.' The chronicler's word, not the miners'. I note it because it is accurate, and because accuracy in metaphor is rarer than accuracy in figures.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578180031_4", anchor: "Niaecum~ has already arranged the contraband routes the Claws will need", text: "The routes. See my annotation on ☽'ofund' Guild. These are almost certainly the mineral-water channels through which the Wake-Singers later breached the Fissured Smelt~ — routes Niaecum~ had handed over in cartographic form, sung into permanent memory beyond retraction. Xouhoulong signed the pact. Niaecum~ drew the map. The guild's motto has never been more precise.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578180031_5", anchor: "that particular green, the shade the elders flinched from", text: "The Long Dark of 4.12. A specific aurora frequency preceded the first mass Walking that season — dozens lost in a single week. Survivors associated the color with the compulsion's onset. That Lianoshing recognizes the flinch without sharing the memory places this detail correctly: Faction Wars, one generation from living witness. Oral tradition doing its work while it still can.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578180031_6", anchor: "three fire-cores fused into her chest-feathers, the old way, before they'd learned that hurt", text: "They knew it hurt. The guild considered the scarring proof of commitment. 'Before they'd learned' is the kind of gentleness chroniclers extend to practices they'd rather not examine.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578180031_7", anchor: "Grand Auditor Qeihong arrived with the efficiency of authority that has finally noticed what it should have prevented", text: "I wrote elsewhere about Qeihong's diagnostic markers — the whisper-guided calculations, the season spent near the Glow-Fissure's taint network, the clinical signs the chroniclers keep framing as political acuity. The efficiency is real. What animates it is not what the reader assumes. One does not investigate corruption with that kind of certainty unless one recognizes it from the inside.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578180031_8", anchor: "he walked toward the sealed southern gallery where the aurora would not go, and he stood before the darkness his architecture had made, and he did not move for a very long time", text: "The chronicler frames this as grief, and perhaps it was only grief. But I cannot read it without hearing the Walking — that compulsion of the Long Dark that seizes those who have lost much, who stand at edges and do not return. Xouhoulong did not walk out onto the ice. He stood before a corridor the aurora refused to enter, and the chronicler does not tell us how long. In the ice-records, the first symptom is always stillness before the walking begins. Not every stillness is the Walking. But I have catalogued enough cases to know that the boundary between mourning and compulsion is visible only afterward, and only to those willing to look.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771578180031_9", anchor: "Each step reasonable. Each step inevitable", text: "Every faction, in every era, believed unprecedented pressures demanded unprecedented compromises. They were not unprecedented. The steps were not inevitable. But I have made this note before, and I will make it again, and it will not help.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
